==============================================================================
sqlxtc -- a networked, threaded SQL engine built on libxtc
==============================================================================

sqlxtc is a worked example that drives a complete, from-scratch SQL
engine entirely on the libxtc runtime.  It began life as a fork of
SQLite whose substrate was progressively re-homed onto xtc primitives;
that migration is now complete, and the SQLite code (sqlite3.c, the
VDBE, and all four extension-point shims) has been removed.  What
remains is a self-contained engine: a Lime-generated SQL parser, a
DuckDB-style vectorized executor, an xtc-native concurrent storage
engine (a B-link tree over a LeanStore-style cooling buffer pool with
an ARIES write-ahead log), and a small multi-client TCP server -- all
written against libxtc and nothing else.

The point of the example is to show libxtc carrying a real, demanding
workload end to end:

  - xtc_app plus a supervisor for process orchestration
  - one xtc_proc per client connection
  - xtc_net for TCP listen/accept with SO_REUSEPORT
  - xtc_exec for an N-loop (N OS threads) executor when run parallel
  - xtc_aio plus xtc_dio_sched for asynchronous, never-blocking page I/O
  - xtc_arwlock for B-link-tree crabbing and for read-mostly catalog state
  - xtc_res for hard caps on memory, file descriptors, and in-flight work
  - xtc_stats for counters and latency histograms
  - xtc_log for structured logging

The wire protocol is "Quack" (see PROTOCOL.md): a deliberately tiny,
line-delimited JSON-over-TCP protocol.  The name is a nod to DuckDB; it
is not interoperable with DuckDB.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Building
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Build libxtc first, then the server:

    cd ../..
    mkdir -p build_unix && cd build_unix && ../dist/configure && make
    cd ../examples/06_sqlxtc
    make

The build produces sqlxtc-server.  There is no SQLite to compile, so
the build is fast.  The committed Lime-generated parser
(sql_parse_gen.c) is compiled directly; you only need the Lime
submodule and "make sql_parse" if you change the grammar
(sql_grammar.lime).

To build against the single-file xtc amalgamation instead of
libxtc.a -- which exercises the amalgamation as a real, broad consumer
of the runtime -- run:

    make amalg

That generates amalg/xtc.c and amalg/include/xtc.h, compiles the one
xtc object, and links sqlxtc-server-amalg against it (no libxtc.a, no
liburing; the amalgamation auto-selects the epoll backend).  The sqlxtc
sources are compiled with -Iamalg/include, where forwarding stub
headers resolve every "#include xtc_*.h" to the single xtc.h.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Running
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Start an in-memory server on the default port 15432 (background it so
the loop is not tied to the shell):

    nohup setsid ./sqlxtc-server -p 15432 -d :memory: \
        < /dev/null > /tmp/sqlxtc.log 2>&1 &
    disown

Talk to it from python.  The server sends a hello banner first, then
one JSON line per result row, terminated by a status line:

    python3 -c '
    import socket, json
    s = socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", 15432), timeout=5)
    f = s.makefile("rwb", buffering=0)
    print(f.readline().decode().strip())                  # hello banner
    f.write(json.dumps({"q": "SELECT 1+1"}).encode() + b"\n")
    for _ in range(3):
        print(f.readline().decode().strip())
    '

Even with -d :memory: the engine keeps its rows in the xstore storage
engine: when --storage is not given, the store file is derived from the
database path (PATH.xstore), or a unique per-process temporary file for
an in-memory database.  Every table lives in xstore; sqlxtc is a SQL
engine over libxtc storage, not a shell over an in-RAM table.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Command-line options
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    sqlxtc-server [options]

      -h, --host=ADDR        bind address           (default 0.0.0.0)
      -p, --port=PORT        bind port              (default 15432)
      -d, --db=PATH          database path          (default :memory:)
      -c, --cores=N          pin to N cores via sched_setaffinity
      -m, --max-memory=N     xtc_res memory cap in bytes; OOM on exceed
      -n, --max-clients=N    reject new connections at the cap (default 1000)
      -i, --max-iops=N       queries/sec token bucket (0 = unlimited)
      -D, --max-databases=N  cap on ATTACHed databases (default 16)
          --storage=PATH     xstore engine file (default: derived from --db)
          --storage-frames=N xstore buffer-pool size in pages (0 = default)
          --storage-direct   open the store with O_DIRECT
          --storage-adaptive adaptive writeback pacing
          --no-shared        give each connection its own handle
      -t, --threads=N        worker loops (default 1; >1 = parallel,
                             connection-per-proc across N OS threads)
      -v, --verbose
          --help


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. How it is put together
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A connection flows like this:

      accept()                              spawn
    listen_fd ----> listener_proc ----xtc_proc----> conn_proc(fd)
                      (xtc_proc)                        |
                                                        v
                                          read a line, quack_parse,
                                          sx_prepare/sx_step (Lime parse
                                          plus vexec over xstore),
                                          quack_emit the row stream
                                                        |
                                                        v
                                              send() back to client

The server opens connections through the sx_ engine facade.  A
connection is a SQLite-free native handle: sx_prepare parses the SQL
with the Lime grammar into an AST, the vectorized executor (vexec) plans
and runs it against the xstore storage engine, and sx_step walks the
result.  There is no VDBE and no virtual-table layer.

When run with -t 1 (the default) everything shares one loop and one
handle.  With -t N (N > 1) an N-loop executor runs N OS threads; the
listener accepts on loop 0 and spawns each connection's proc
round-robin across loops, so SQL runs in parallel across cores.
Connections share data through the one process-global xstore engine
(its B-link tree, buffer pool, commit clock, and WAL are shared); the
storage engine's latches and the WAL group-commit writer provide the
concurrency, so there is no global handle mutex on the hot path.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Source layout (server and engine)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Server / protocol
    main.c       argument parsing, app and supervisor bringup, listener
    conn.c       per-connection xtc_proc; reads lines, dispatches SQL
    quack.c      hand-rolled JSON encoder/decoder for the Quack protocol
    db.c         connection lifecycle and result streaming via Quack
    admin.c      SHOW PROCESSES and other introspection commands
    metrics.c    periodic xtc_res / xtc_stats snapshot logger

  SQL front end
    sql_grammar.lime   the Lime grammar for the accepted SQL subset
    sql_parse_gen.c    the committed Lime-generated parser tables
    sql_parse_drv.c    the push-parser driver (tokens -> AST)
    sql_ast.c          the AST node arena and constructors
    sql_parse.c        the lexer / pre-classifier
    vexec.c            the vectorized executor (scan, filter, project,
                       aggregate, sort, hash join, subquery, DDL/DML)

  Engine facade and storage
    engine.c     the sx_ facade: open/prepare/step/finalize, the native
                 driver, statement classification, transactions
    xstore.c     the catalog, the MVCC record store, snapshot isolation
                 and SSI, the native transaction / DDL / scan APIs
    sqlrec.c     the row record codec (encode / decode of a tuple)
    btree.c      the B-link tree (latch crabbing, splits, merges)
    btnode.c     the slotted node with prefix compression
    bufmgr.c     the cooling buffer pool (CLOCK eviction, double-write
                 torn-page protection, async page I/O via xtc_aio)
    wal.c        the write-ahead log and group-commit writer
    xlog.c       the ARIES log-record codec
    mvcc.c       the sharded MVCC / 2PC scale-out experiment (standalone)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. The storage engine
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rows live in a from-scratch, xtc-native storage engine, not in any
borrowed B-tree.  The major pieces:

  bufmgr.c -- a page-id-addressed buffer manager.  A fix resolves a page
  id to a resident, pinned frame through a striped-lock page table,
  faulting the page in on a miss, with a cooling-stage CLOCK evictor.  A
  page-provider process (an xtc_proc) cools cold frames and writes the
  dirty ones out ahead of demand, so reclaiming a frame is a cheap state
  flip plus a page-table removal.  Page reads and writes are submitted
  to xtc_aio (io_uring on Linux, kqueue file-AIO on the BSDs), so the
  reactor thread never blocks on disk; the calling fiber parks until the
  completion arrives and the loop keeps serving peers.  A double-write
  buffer protects against torn pages on crash.

  btnode.c -- a slotted B-tree node with prefix compression: the common
  prefix of a page's fence keys is stored once and each slot keeps only
  the key suffix plus a 4-byte head for fast comparison.

  btree.c -- a B-link tree.  Concurrent writers descend with latch
  crabbing (an xtc_arwlock per node), and the B-link right-links let a
  reader that arrives mid-split follow the link rather than block, so
  reads and writes proceed concurrently.

  wal.c / xlog.c -- an ARIES-style write-ahead log.  Each commit logs
  its changes durably before they reach the tree; a group-commit writer
  coalesces many committers' fsyncs into one.  Recovery does the
  analysis / redo / undo passes and writes compensation log records.

  xstore.c -- the catalog and the MVCC record store on top of the tree.
  It provides snapshot isolation, serializable snapshot isolation (SSI)
  write-skew detection, in-transaction read-your-writes, savepoints, and
  the native transaction / DDL / scan APIs the executor calls.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Testing
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The in-process tests build small drivers against the engine objects and
run without a daemon:

    make test                 # the full in-process test aggregate

The authoritative correctness proof is an external differential oracle:
it runs a corpus of queries against sqlxtc-server and against python's
sqlite3, and fails on any divergence.  It is independent of any SQLite
code linked into sqlxtc (there is none):

    bash ../../test/sqlxtc/test_sqlxtc_oracle.sh

A multi-thread load test drives many concurrent clients against a
parallel server:

    bash ../../test/sqlxtc/test_sqlxtc_mt.sh

Continuous integration runs the in-process tests, the amalgamation
build, the Lime-parser regeneration check (the committed parser must be
byte-stable), the multi-thread load test, and the differential oracle.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. Performance
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

See ../../bench/sqlxtc/ for benchmark drivers and recorded results
(SQLITE_VS_SQLXTC.md compares the native executor against the historical
VDBE path on the same stored data; LIBXTC_USAGE.md maps each subsystem
to the libxtc primitive it uses).  In broad strokes: GROUP BY
aggregation is faster than the old VDBE path and scales with row count;
scans are at parity; the executor's morsel-parallel reads use xtc_exec.

Read-heavy concurrent load scales past the single-loop ceiling with
-t N.  Write throughput serializes at the WAL group-commit writer and
the B-link-tree latches rather than at any global handle mutex.


